t fraction w as mar kedly reduced following the LGI diet (8.57%).Observ ations in the LGI group coincided with changes in blood glycaemic biomarkers, including a significant reduction in HOMA-IR (␀ 1.78, p = 0.043). || Additionally, the LGI diet resulted in significant reductions in body weight (␀ 1.2 kg, p = 0.018) and body mass index (␀ 0.38 kg/ m 2 , p = 0.017), as well as increased pre-meal appetite scores during the middle of the diet period and decreased interleukin 6; || Patients with MASLD often consume high GI (HGI) foods, which are associated with insulin resistance, inflammation, metabolic dysfunction, and liver damag e [7,9]. || LGI diets have been proposed as an effective lifestyle intervention in MASLD due to their potential ability to reduce circulating glucose spikes, insulin resistance, hepatic glucose influx, and de novo lipogenesis [11]. || High-fibre diets, how ever, are not always associated with LGI, as some fibre types can have a lesser impact on postprandial glycemia and hyperinsulinemia [14].","Original article The effect of a two-w eek low glycaemic index, higher fibre diet versus high glycaemic index diet on body composition, ectopic lipids, inflammatory biomarkers, gastrointestinal hormones and gut microbiota in metabolic dysfunction-associated steatotic liver disease (MASLD): A pilot randomized cross-over study Amina A. Al-Aw adi a,b,c,d,1 , Jane I. Grove a,b , Stephen Bawden b,e,f,g , Amrita Vijay b,h , Ana M. Valdes b,h , Penny Gow land b,e , Moir a A. Taylor b,i,2 , Guruprasad P. Aithal a,b,*,2 a Nottingham Digestive Diseases Centre, Translational Medical Sciences, School of Medicine, University of Nottingham, Nottingham, UK b National Institute for Health Research (NIHR) No",ok,
